#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# log_gamma

#% text
# `log_gamma(x)` gives the logarithm of the gamma function of `x`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# For `x > 0` the value is the real `log(gamma(x))`; for complex and negative arguments it is the principal branch of DLMF 5.4, analytic off the negative real axis, which can differ from `log(gamma(x))` by a multiple of `2*pi*im`.

#% item
# `log_gamma` does not overflow where `gamma` does: it is the way to work with factorials of large numbers.

#% item
# Positive integers give exact values; machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values.

#% item
# `log_gamma` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
log_gamma(5)
#% output
#= log(24)

#% cell
log_gamma(10.5)
#% output
#= 13.940625219403763

#% text
# 30 digits:

#% cell
n(log_gamma(1/3), 30)
#% output
#= 0.9854206469277670691871740369779614

#% text
# Far beyond the range of machine numbers for `gamma`:

#% cell
log_gamma(1.0e6)
#% output
#= 12815504.569147611

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A negative non-integer argument gives a complex value:

#% cell
log_gamma(-2.5)
#% output
#= -0.056243716497674054 - 9.42477796076938im

#% text
# A complex machine number:

#% cell
log_gamma(2.0 + 3.0*im)
#% output
#= -2.092851753092731 + 2.302396543466868im

#% text
# Each element of a list:

#% cell
log_gamma([1.0, 2.0, 3.0])
#% output
#= [0.0, 0.0, 0.6931471805599453]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The derivative is `digamma`:

#% cell
d(log_gamma(x), x)
#% output
#= digamma(x)

#% text
# For a positive argument it agrees with `log(gamma(x))`:

#% cell
log_gamma(7.3) - log(gamma(7.3))
#% output
#= 0.0

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# `gamma` overflows long before `log_gamma` does:

#% cell
gamma(200.0)
#% output
#= inf

#% cell
log_gamma(200.0)
#% output
#= 857.9336698258575

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `gamma`, `digamma`, `polygamma`, `log_barnes_g`, `factorial`
